// CLAIM: Printing a String stops at a zero byte
// HOLDS: accepted
// WHY: a String is not NUL-terminated — `len` is one load from the eight bytes before the
//      first byte, so `len(from_bytes([65, 0, 66]))` is 3 and the value genuinely holds
//      three bytes. But every print path formats a String with `%s`, which stops at the
//      first zero. Measured 2026-08-21 in BOTH compilers, identically: the program below
//      prints `A`, not `A`, a zero byte, `B`. `print`, `print_exact` and interpolation all
//      do it, because they share one formatter — which is why they agree.
//
//      The exact write is `fwrite(ptr, 1, len, stdout)` and it is NOT done, deliberately:
//      `stdout` is a libc DATA symbol that Darwin spells `__stdoutp`, so the fix costs a
//      second renamed symbol in `stamp_target` and a matching one in stage-1 — which emits
//      `dprintf(2, ...)` precisely to avoid needing a `stderr` global at all. That is real
//      portability surface for a case nothing here reaches: `print_exact` was added for LSP
//      framing, and JSON may not contain a raw zero byte.
//
//      This file exists so the limit cannot become folklore. If it is ever fixed, the fix is
//      a new symbol in two compilers and this file is the record of what it buys. And the
//      inverse claim is the one worth re-reading: `len` is right, the WRITE is short — so a
//      program that declares a length from `len` and then prints the text writes fewer bytes
//      than it promised.
//
//      **The framing above was too narrow when it was written, and BMX said so.** "A declared
//      length that lies" is one trap; the reachable one had no length in it at all.
//      `lib/json.bx` escaped seven characters and passed the other twenty-five control bytes
//      through raw, so `json_render` promised valid JSON and delivered invalid JSON — and for
//      a zero byte, a string that never closed. **That is fixed** (every byte below 0x20 is
//      now `\u00XX`, checked against Python's `json`), which is what makes this limit narrow
//      rather than merely documented: the cheap half was in `lib/`, not in a libc symbol.
//      What still reaches it is text built from arbitrary bytes and printed directly, such as
//      rendering a document into a page.
//
//      **The byte paths are faithful, and that is measured rather than assumed** — it is the
//      answer a reader needs and it was advice until 2026-08-21. `write_bytes` wrote 64 bytes
//      holding 16 zeros and `read_file` returned all 64 exactly. The star-burxt session measured
//      the two paths that carry real binary: `file_write` restored a `.vsix` (685 zero bytes)
//      byte-for-byte with a matching digest, and `net_write`/`http_serve` served an 83,629-byte
//      wasm module (11,214 zero bytes) and fetched it back with a matching digest, across the
//      partial-write path. The reason is the whole distinction: those take an explicit LENGTH,
//      and a print is null-terminated by the format string it passes through.
//
//      **So: serve and store binary freely; do not `print` it.**
use "../../lib/string.bx";

region r {
    let s: String = from_bytes([65, 0, 66]);
    // Three, and it is the printing that is short — not the String.
    print(len(s));
    print_exact(s);
    print("");
}
